  "result": "Neither five nor ten. The premise is the problem: more agents does not always give better results.\n\nAnthropic measured this on their own multi-agent research system. Agents used about 4× the tokens of a plain chat, and multi-agent systems about 15×. Each extra agent re-reads its own context, and the agents spend tokens passing work back and forth. Their multi-agent setup beat a single agent by 90.2% on their internal research eval. They say that pays off only for high-value tasks that split into parallel pieces, need more than one context window, or use many tools ([source](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system)). Your sponsor's budget is small, so that cost matters.\n\nTake a student asking \"Can I book the laser cutter Thursday, and what material is safe?\" One agent can do it: look up the manual page, check the calendar, book the slot. Splitting it into a manual agent, a calendar agent, a booking agent and a router adds hand-offs. Each hand-off can drop details, such as which student is asking, and each one adds cost. Ten agents would multiply that.\n\nA better design question is \"what is the fewest moving parts that works?\" Start with one agent and a few well-described tools. Make the fixed steps, like the booking confirmation, a plain workflow in code. Split off a second agent only when you can name a reason: a task that really runs in parallel, or a context that no longer fits.\n\nQuick check: for the request above, which step would you keep as a fixed workflow rather than let an agent decide, and why?\n\nIf you want, I can write a lesson on workflows versus agents and when to split. That is the argument you'll need at your design review.",
